# A HOUSE ON A SLOPE SITS ON ITS OWN TERRACE — plan 20 `A4`.
#
# ⚠ THE COUNT AND THE PICTURE ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS. `lib/hex_editor/tests/seat_pad.loft`
# owns the numbers — the seat is the footprint's own mean, the cut balances the fill,
# the residual is reported. What only a picture can say is whether the result reads as
# a building standing on ground, rather than as one sunk into it.
#
# ⚠ AND THE CONTROL IS A HOUSE ON THE FLAT, shot first. Seating on the footprint has to
# be a NO-OP where the footprint's own terrain is the author's grade — otherwise every
# house in every world already built moves — so the flat case is the row that guards
# everything already made.

echo --- CONTROL: a house on flat ground, which must be exactly as it always was
at 0 0 30
step 20
key H
wait rebuilt
step 30
at -6 1 0
step 40
snap a4-control-flat
frame 0 1.0 masonry 0.03 1.0 roof 0.01 1.0

echo --- now a hill, and a house on its FLANK
# ⚠ THE AUTHOR STANDS OFF THE PEAK AND LOOKS ACROSS IT, so the footprint lands on
# ground that is several cells of gradient away from their feet. That gap IS the
# defect this step removed: the pad used to be seated at the height under the author,
# which on this slope is a different number entirely.
at 40 0 30
step 20
key ArrowUp
key ArrowUp
key ArrowUp
key ArrowUp
wait rebuilt
step 30

echo --- stand on the flank and place it
at 46 0 30
step 30
key H
wait rebuilt
step 40

echo --- and look at it from DOWNHILL, where the cut and the fill are both in frame
# ⚠ FROM BELOW AND CLOSE. From above a pad is a rectangle and any seat looks the same;
# the claim is about what happens at the WALL, so the shot has to have the wall's foot
# in it. The dark band under the uphill side is the cut, and `A5` draws it as rock the
# moment it passes what grass can stand.
at 34 3 0
send 40:1
step 40
snap a4-house-on-a-flank
frame 0 1.0 masonry 0.02 1.0

echo --- and from the uphill side, which is where a house used to be buried
at 64 3 180
step 40
snap a4-from-uphill
frame 0 1.0 masonry 0.02 1.0
